---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { profile } from '../data/profile';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '../lib/jsonld';

const title = `Engineering with agents — ${profile.name}`;
const description =
  'Harnesses for platforms AI agents cannot reach, the knowledge base they ' +
  'answer from, and the pipelines that take a defect ticket through fix, test ' +
  'and review.';
---

<Base
  {title}
  {description}
  cwd="~"
  jsonLd={breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Agents', path: '/agents/' },
  ])}
>
  <span slot="command">cat agents.md</span>

  <h1>Engineering with agents</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Since 2025 most of my work has been making AI agents genuinely useful on
    products they could not touch before. Three things had to be true at once,
    and none of them are prompt engineering.
  </p>

  <ol class="pillars">
    <li>
      <span class="pillars__n">01</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Reach</h2>
        <p>
          An agent that cannot reach the platform cannot do the work. I build
          harnesses that provision and drive <strong>Windows virtual
          machines</strong> so agents can build a two-decade-old desktop CRM and
          validate tests against it, and I
          instrument <strong>physical iPad hardware</strong> so they can install
          and debug iOS builds.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="pillars__n">02</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Context</h2>
        <p>
          Reach without context is guesswork. A <strong>central knowledge
          base</strong> covers every product in the portfolio, consolidating
          documentation inherited through acquisition into one structured source,
          and it is wired into the agents so they answer from real product
          knowledge. It cut onboarding onto an unfamiliar product
          by <strong>roughly 70 percent</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          I wrote the <strong>bulk migration tool</strong> that moves legacy
          documents in with AI-proposed structure, and
          the <strong>per-product context layer</strong> each agent loads before
          it touches a codebase.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="pillars__n">03</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Trust</h2>
        <p>
          An agent will happily write a regression test that passes against the
          broken code, which proves nothing. So every test has to
          be <strong>observed failing</strong> before the fix goes back in,
          enforced by a <strong>validation agent</strong> that rejects any test
          never seen to fail.
        </p>
        <p>
          Without that check the throughput is worth nothing, because you cannot
          tell a fixed defect from one that was merely closed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 class="std">What runs on top</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Serverless pipelines</strong> take an incoming defect ticket,
    reproduce the failure, <strong>analyse crash dumps on remote machines</strong>,
    implement a fix, open a pull request, run the tests and review the result.
    Engineers handle only what the agents cannot resolve.
  </p>

  <h2 class="std">What changed</h2>
  <p>
    My own merged output went from <strong>41 changes in the second half of
    2024</strong>, to <strong>131 in 2025</strong>, to <strong>179 so far in
    2026</strong>, while closing <strong>580 of 591</strong> assigned defects.
    The pipelines are <strong>used across the team</strong> now rather than just
    by me.
  </p>

  <p class="more mono"><a href="/work/agent-maintenance/">full case study →</a></p>

  <style>
    .lede {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: var(--step-1);
      color: var(--text-dim);
      max-width: 52ch;
      text-wrap: pretty;
    }
    p { color: var(--text-dim); }
    p strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }

    .pillars {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 2rem 0;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      gap: 1px;
      background: var(--border);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
    }
    .pillars li {
      background: var(--bg);
      padding: 1.4rem 1.25rem;
      display: flex;
      gap: 1rem;
      align-items: flex-start;
    }
    .pillars__n {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: var(--step--1);
      color: var(--accent);
      flex: 0 0 auto;
      padding-top: 0.35em;
    }
    .pillars h2 {
      font-size: var(--step-1);
      margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
      letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    }
    .pillars p {
      margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
      font-size: var(--step--1);
      line-height: 1.7;
    }
    .pillars p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

    .std { margin-top: 2.75rem; }
    .more { margin-top: 2.5rem; font-size: var(--step--1); }
    .more a { text-decoration: none; color: var(--accent); }
    .more a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  </style>
</Base>
